# Git blogs: generic scenarios only

Teaching copy and diagrams must stand alone. Do not explain concepts through the planted lab story, challenge IDs, or repo-specific plot.

## Do not use in body, lede, figures, or callouts

- Challenge / lab plot details: sprint service, order-processor, flag fix, agentic branch, board-summary hotfix, release/2.1 Ops deploy, leaked env, etc.
- Challenge-flavored file or config names: `feature_login_default`, planted paths unique to a lab.
- **Lab-matching numbers and setups** a student could copy-paste: same tag/version (`v2.2.0`, `hotfix/2.1.1`), ticket prefix (`FB-`), conflict values (`channel = slack|email`), branch/doc names that are one-word swaps of the lab, identical hook regexes, or the same commit subjects as graders.
- Phrases that assume the reader is inside a challenge: “in this lab you will…”, “the labs make you prove…”, “after these labs you are ready for the capstone…” inside concept sections.
- Wiring teaching examples to `gatedByChallengeId` / related-lab labels beyond a short, generic “Related labs” / “Practice next” pointer.

## Use instead

- Generic team situations: “a shared project on GitHub”, “you and a teammate”, “a bug fix while someone else ships a feature”, “a release branch vs main”.
- Neutral names in snippets with **different** values from the labs: `my-project`, `fix/rollout-guard`, `settings/flags.toml`, `release/3.4`, `v4.0.0` / `hotfix/3.9.1`, `TKT-42` — teach the pattern, not the lab answer.
- Point to labs only at the edge: Related labs links, or one sentence like “Practice this loop in the next lab” with no challenge plot.

## Examples

```text
❌ Someone flipped `feature_login_default` to `full` on main — rescue it for the flag-fix lab…
✅ A bad default landed on `main`. Open a short-lived fix branch so deployable `main` stays clean while you repair it.

❌ In the next lab you land tonight’s board-summary hotfix on `release/2.1` so Ops can deploy…
✅ Cherry-pick lets you copy one commit onto a release branch without bringing every unreleased change from `main`.

❌ Blog uses `git tag -a v2.2.0` and `hotfix/2.1.1` when the lab grades those exact names…
✅ Blog uses `v4.0.0` / `hotfix/3.9.1`; labs keep their own versions.

❌ These ideas show up on the planted order-processor repo…
✅ (Omit from concept copy; leave planting to the lab brief.)
```

`relatedLabs` / `gatedByChallengeId` metadata may still reference challenge IDs — that is wiring, not teaching narrative.
